<Admonition type="tip" title="Disclosure">

- I'm a member of the Betaflight development team, I have access to direct manufacturer communication, including SpeedyBee. However, everything in this article will be sourced from public information.
- SpeedyBee nor RateFPV have any oversight over this article. I did receive SpeedyBee products in the past as hardware samples for official approval. But I'm not influenced by either one of them in writing this.

</Admonition>

# Background

I probably don't need to introduce SpeedyBee, but just so that everything is on the same page. SpeedyBee is a well-known manufacturer of basically everything FPV at this point.

Rate, however, is much less known. I've come across their products around a year ago, when they had a few electronics and a small BNF available on Banggood. Over time, they've expanded a lot across the whole FPV space, and now are seemingly inching into the collective consciousness of the FPV community.

Seemingly, as they're becoming more popular, some rumors are starting to spread. I would like to at least try to set things straight.

# The Rumor

## Reddit, my involvement

I noticed this floating around a few weeks back. A reddit comment here and there, a few messages on Discord. Some of which I replied to myself in increasing bewilderment:

> [That makes sense, iv also heard that speedybee got in trouble with the chineese gov, and they are now speculated to be ratefpv.com](https://www.reddit.com/r/fpv/comments/1w2pjqs/comment/p6udmbl/)
>
> > As a member of the BF dev team, I've seen nothing to even suggest this. We have relatively good comms with SpeedyBee, their new stuff is reviewed and officially supported. Rate don't have a single officially supported product in ELRS, AM32 or Betaflight, and some of their FCs/AIOs use MCUs that Betaflight doesn't even support.

If you don't want to read further, this might be reason enough. But let's go a bit deeper. Where did this actually start?

## Earliest Mention - CultureFPV

From what I've been able to find, the first mention of a possible connection between them comes from a French website - https://culturefpv.fr. They posted an article about the [RateFPV F4 40A AIO](https://culturefpv.fr/aio-ratefpv-f4-40a-20260720/) on July 20th 2026:

> Et la ressemblance ne s’arrête pas aux specs : visuellement, la RateFPV rappelle une SpeedyBee de façon assez frappante, du dessin du dissipateur à l’agencement général de la carte. Sur la fiche aussi les deux sont très proches : format compact, 40A, Bluetooth, 3-6S, cible petits moteurs, et même une plaque d’entraînement à la soudure dans la boîte.

Which translates to:

> And the similarities don’t stop at the specs: visually, the RateFPV bears a striking resemblance to a SpeedyBee, from the heat sink design to the overall layout of the board. On paper, too, the two are very similar: compact size, 40A, Bluetooth, 3-6S, designed for small motors, and even a soldering practice board included in the box.

## Joshua Bardwell's Livestream

But the biggest spread of the rumor likely came from Joshua Bardwell, specifically the [Q&A Livestream - August 30, 2026](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rVNh2YT1sds). Around the 1:26:00, after a prior topic of SpeedyBee hardware not being discontinued/not available, a chatter directly proclaims that Rate is SpeedyBee in all caps. Other chat members, including more prominent names in the community like CiottiFPV agree. JB pulls Rate's website up on the stream, and talks about it being completely normal:

> The chat is suggesting that RateFPV is SpeedyBee. [RateFPV's website shown in the background] That's the other thing that happens - the company gets taken down, the CEO goes to prison or gets a fine, and then the company starts up again under a different name. Welcome to China. ["RateFPV F4V4 Mini Stack" page is open] Is RateFPV SpeedyBee? That'd be great. 50 bucks for an F405 stack. **_RATEFPV!!!! THE NEW SPEEDYBEE-_** _don't say that. You'll get in trouble_.

JB didn't outright confirm or deny it, but definitely seemed to entertain the idea, with chat being much more enthusiastic about it.

# Not SpeedyBee

Now that we know where it came from, let's look into the possible connections, and then disprove them one by one.

It is true that SpeedyBee has been kind of struggling lately, with regulatory pressure from both the Chinese and US governments, and rampant cloning of their F405 V3 and V4 boards. As an aside... horrible idea to clone the F405 V3, the design is messed up to the point where making it better would break it. But nontheless, SpeedyBee hasn't had a great time.

It is also true that FPV companies split apart pretty often, the most famous example probably being Radiomaster splitting off from Jumper, now there's also HelloRadioSky splitting off from RadioMaster, so now we're two layers of separation down from Jumper, who started off by offering Futaba-adjacent radios. AxisFlying split from T-Motor, and DeepspaceFPV split from Axis.

But those are all essentially clean separations into new standalone brands/teams/companies, not a rebranding to get around regulatory issues.

## Paper Trail

The main motivation for the rumor seems to be that SpeedyBee is struggling to get their boards out, so they're going through a shell company to get around regulatory issues. Rate would be a new company that exploded onto the market with a bunch of products all at once after SpeedyBee started running into trouble, and we might see a paper trail connecting SpeedyBee to Rate. But that's not the case here, like... at all.

### Dates

The RateFPV website has been registered since the 26th of August 2024. The "RATEFPV" trademark was applied for on the 6th of February 2026, and the "Shenzhen Zhizhi rotor technology co., LTD" company that filed for it was established on the 23rd of January 2026.

