  <figcaption>Company registration</figcaption>
 </figure>
</div>

While the legal stuff is fairly recent, there clearly was something here long before the current US monarchy came into power, and before trade relations started to break down like they are now. There are youtube reviews of Rate products going back to October 2025, and random videos using Rate-branded hardware as far back as August 2024:

> Rimzler - [The Best Value 3" FPV Drone! Rate S30Pro](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1vDZK1bJ31Q) (5. 10. 2025)

> xiang ge - [Rate S30 freestyle at CAAS' 2nd Dedicated Area for drone](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dmXT-rKJfEQ) (17. 8. 2024)

### Ownership

We already covered the company behind RateFPV, and their trademark application. But we can dig a little deeper. Their website lists "BLUE STAR TRADING LIMITED", a Hong Kong entity, for its contact information and legal purposes. That company is listed as a contact detail for multiple unrelated stores, so they most likely just act as a cross-border merchant or logistics provider. Rate themselves are registered (and operate/ship from) Shenzhen, China.

SpeedyBee is a completely different story. Their main operator is registered as "Zhuhai KuaiFeng Technology Co., Ltd", with a secondary entity called "LangTian Technology (Shenzhen) Co., Ltd" being responsible for their privacy policy and Betaflight manufacturer registration. Their US trademark being attributed to "RunCam Technology (Shenzhen) Co., Ltd".

There's stronger evidence connecting SpeedyBee to RunCam, Oxbot, and CoreWing than there is connecting them to RateFPV, but that's maybe for another article.

## Firmware Support

I won't share any internal communication or private information from both companies, the aim of this article is to provide everything based on information that is publicly available. But even the public side shows what's going on pretty clearly, and not just within Betaflight.

On one side, there is SpeedyBee with 16 Betaflight targets, a bunch of their latest one being officially verified and supported. On the other side, Rate have no firmware targets merged or waiting in a pull request, they provide firmware support themselves.

Same goes for ELRS, SpeedyBee has 3 firmware targets, two for standalone receivers, and one for their AIO. Rate again have 0 official targets.

This shows a clear difference in the level of involvement with the community and open source projects, with SpeedyBee having a good track record of "playing by the rules" and Rate not being nearly as involved.

## Product (Dis)Similarities

If we can't draw a connection between them on paper or their open source connections, let's see if there's anything at the hardware level.

Rate have a pretty impressive lineup of products for a relatively new company. A 65A 30.5x30.5 stack and a 40A 20x20 stack, both with F405/F435/F722 FC MCUs... a lot to do basically the same thing lol, but that's beside the point. To top it off, an F435 40A 25.5x25.5 AIO. I suppose that you could say that's similar to SpeedyBee's offerings on the surface level, but by those measures, every single manufacturer out there with F4/F7 stacks and AIOs would be a SpeedyBee shell company.

If that's not convincing enough, let's look deeper into the actual products.

### Big Stack

RateFPV [F4V4 65A Stack](https://ratefpv.com/products/ratefpv-f4v4-f435-55-65a-stack?variant=53633849688432) vs SpeedyBee [F405 V4 Stack](https://www.speedybee.com/speedybee-f405-v4-bls-60a-30x30-fc-esc-stack/)/[F405 V5 Stack](https://www.speedybee.com/speedybee-f405-v5-55a-stack/)

At the surface, the specs are pretty close. Size, MCUs, ESC specs, even Bluetooth. We'll find the biggest difference in the board design. It's fair to say that SpeedyBee has a pretty distinct design language, with the prominent cutouts/protrusions in the front and rear of their boards respectively. Even though they moved a bit further from that with the V5, there are other parallels we can draw. The FCs all have components on both sides, have the prominently red BLE antennas and battery indicator LEDs right next to each other driven by the Espressif MCU, and all the ESCs have the pads coming straight out, with beefy heatsinks and no FC coverings.

