### Small Stack

RateFPV [F4V4 40A Mini Stack](https://ratefpv.com/products/ratefpv-f4v4-f435-40a-mini-stack-for-fpv-racing-drones) vs [SpeedyBee F405 Mini Stack](https://www.speedybee.com/speedybee-f405-mini-bls-35a-20x20-stack/)

Again, we have similar specs to start off with. 35A is reasonably close to 40A, similar MCUs, even BLE (this time easily identifiable on the Rate stack). The Rate stack uses 32bit ESC firmware while SpeedyBee only offers 8bit, and that's where the similarities end.

<div class="article-image-grid grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
	<figure>
		<a href="/uploads/articles/rate-isnt-speedybee/small-stack/rate-small-stack.webp"><img src="/uploads/articles/rate-isnt-speedybee/small-stack/rate-small-stack.webp" alt="RateFPV F4V4 40A Mini Stack" loading="lazy" /></a>
		<figcaption>RateFPV F4V4 40A Mini Stack</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<figure>
		<a href="/uploads/articles/rate-isnt-speedybee/small-stack/sb-small-stack.webp"><img src="/uploads/articles/rate-isnt-speedybee/small-stack/sb-small-stack.webp" alt="SpeedyBee F405 Mini Stack" loading="lazy" /></a>
		<figcaption>SpeedyBee F405 Mini Stack</figcaption>
	</figure>
</div>

SpeedyBee's FC and ESC both have that familiar cutout thingy which Rate surprisingly does too... but in the opposite side for some reason. Both FCs have components on both sides, but the pad layout is completely different with SB keeping it in front and back rows, and Rate going all around the board. The MCUs are on different sides, and Rate offers two additional connectors. They even went to pretty great lengths to source black JST-SH connectors, while SpeedyBee uses white ones.

I think the most interesting thing here are the small boards that house the OSD and BLE circuitry. SB integrates everything directly, Rate seem to be re-using existing modules. We'll get back to this further down in the article ;)

