	</figure>
	<figure>
		<a href="/uploads/articles/rate-isnt-speedybee/aio/rate-aio-bottom-bare.webp"><img src="/uploads/articles/rate-isnt-speedybee/aio/rate-aio-bottom-bare.webp" alt="RateFPV AIO underside showing four ESC chips and connectors" loading="lazy" /></a>
		<figcaption>RateFPV AIO, bottom</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<figure>
		<a href="/uploads/articles/rate-isnt-speedybee/aio/sb-aio-bottom.webp"><img src="/uploads/articles/rate-isnt-speedybee/aio/sb-aio-bottom.webp" alt="SpeedyBee AIO underside with its second PCB and USB connector" loading="lazy" /></a>
		<figcaption>SpeedyBee AIO, bottom</figcaption>
	</figure>
</div>

I've heard the argument in favor of them being one and the same being that the pad layout is similar. But there's only so many ways that you can arrange IO pads on such a tight yet powerful board. Like here for example, these are boards that predate both Rate and SpeedyBee's current offerings (as far as I know), and they all have a similar layout - USB close to the battery pads, motor pads tucked towards the left and right mounting holes (assuming a diagonal mounting orientation), and IO pads on the front corner on both the left and right sides. Going by this definition, we'd have a ring of shell companies.

